# Red Hat AI Design Language Standards

Follow these standards whenever implementing AI-related UI features. Source: Red Hat Brand & Experience Design Team deck (March 2026).

## Core Principles

1. **Be transparent** — always make it clear when and how AI is being used. Err on the side of over-communicating.
2. **Personable, not human** — AI follows Red Hat voice and tone but never pretends the user is talking to a human.
3. **Stay within the design system** — AI features must use PatternFly and Red Hat Design System standards. No special styling that sets AI apart from the rest of the product.

## Transparency Notices (Required)

- Every AI-enabled interaction needs **at minimum one visual AND one verbal indicator** (e.g., sparkle icon + "with AI" button text).
- Place the icon **before** the disclosure text. No tooltips on disclosure messages.
- AI-generated content (summaries, search results) must include a label + icon (e.g., "AI-assisted" heading with sparkle icon).
- For AI-assisted features beyond basic labeling, add a **text notice at the start** of the experience.
- For virtual assistants / chatbots:
  - Before interaction: *"This feature uses AI technology. Do not include any personal information or other sensitive information in your input."*
  - Persistent notice near input/output: *"Always review AI-generated content prior to use."*
- Consider additional indicators for high-risk interactions (consult AIA Reviewers).

## Iconography

Use the official Red Hat AI sparkle UI icons. Do NOT create custom AI icons.

**Information icons:** `ai-info`, `ai-error`, `ai-experience`
**Action icons:** `ai-edit`, `ai-troubleshoot`, `ai-filter`, `ai-search`, `ai-create`, `ai-enhance`

Rules:
- Always **pair AI icons with text** ("...with AI", "...by AI", or similar). Icon alone is not enough.
- Do NOT use brains or robots for AI features (robots are reserved for chatbot avatars only).
- Do NOT change AI icon colors or modify them — use the same colors as other UI icons in the interface.
- Do NOT use AI sparkle icons without an accompanying AI disclosure.
- Do NOT use AI sparkle to mean "new" — use `rh-ui-icon-new` instead.
- Do NOT add sparkles to technology icons.

## Color

- **Do NOT use color or gradients to indicate AI.** AI features use the exact same color tokens as non-AI interface elements.
- Do NOT color-code AI features differently from the rest of the UI. This conflicts with established status colors (red=danger, green=success, blue=interactive, etc.).
- No gradient fills or gradient borders on AI elements.

## Chatbot Avatars

- All chatbots use the **Red Hat robot icon** as their avatar. No other icons, no custom robots.
- Robot variations (happy, annoyed, bewildered, sad) exist for personality — do NOT use them for state changes or alerts.
- Use PatternFly **non-status color tokens** for avatar styling. Avoid `success-green`, `interaction-blue`, and `danger-red`.
- Launch buttons for chatbots use the robot icon with PatternFly icon button variants (primary, secondary, inverse depending on context).
- Do NOT use gradient fills/borders on chatbot launch buttons.

## Animation

- Use the **premade sparkle animation** only — triggered on hover, rollover, or click.
- Loop **once**, then return to rest state. The AI sparkle must be visible at rest.
- Do NOT auto-play or endlessly loop animations.
- Animation stays within the **same bounding box** as the static icon (no padding changes).
- Do NOT create exaggerated or over-the-top animations.

## Compliance Reminder

AI design guidance does not replace AI Assessments, Privacy Impact Assessments, or other required internal reviews. For external-facing AI features, always consult AIA Reviewers.
